---
id: org.dxos.app-framework.edge-plugins
name: EdgePlugins
version: 0.1.0
---

The Composer plugins that run in the browser also run on EDGE. A plugin is defined once, in its
`dx.config.ts` with `Plugin.config`, and built once: its vite build emits one bundle and one
manifest for every runtime. The CLI publishes that bundle to the registry; the browser loads it as
it does today, and EDGE loads the same bundle on demand into a Dynamic Worker through the Worker
Loader API. Its ECHO types, skills and operations become visible in the registry, and its
operations are callable through compute-service like any other EDGE operation.

Today a plugin reaches EDGE only by being compiled into operation-service: a static list of
`@dxos/plugin-*` imports, activated by one `PluginManager` per isolate and republished as a KV
catalog. Adding or changing a plugin needs a dxos catalog bump and a redeploy of a 12 MiB worker,
plugins from outside the dxos repository cannot run on EDGE at all, and every plugin shares one
isolate. This spec replaces that worker with plugin bundles that are stored, versioned and loaded
independently.

The design reuses three things that already exist:

- **The plugin manifest** (`Plugin.Manifest`, `Plugin.fromManifest`). A `Plugin.config` evaluates at
  build time to data: plugin metadata plus one `ManifestModule` per module, with capability tags as
  `{identifier, arity}`, activation events as data, one URL per module body and the module's
  `environments`. At load, `Plugin.fromModules` keeps only the modules whose `environments` include
  the runtime's `Environment.current`, so the same manifest serves the browser and EDGE.
- **The registry** (registry-service). `/upload` already stores immutable plugin bundles in R2 under
  `modules/<slug>/<version>/` with their `manifest.json`, keeps private releases in D1, and indexes
  public ones from ATProto records. The bundle EDGE runs is that bundle.
- **Worker Loader in compute-service** (`WorkerLoaderRuntime`, the code-mode script runner). Bundles
  in R2 are already loaded into Dynamic Workers with space-agnostic service stubs in `env`, loopback
  entrypoints from `ctx.exports` for per-caller services, and a tail worker for logs.

## Loading in the browser and on EDGE

One bundle runs in both places, but the two load it differently, because they share packages
differently.

- **Browser.** Every plugin runs in one JS context with Composer. A plugin bundle keeps every
  package Composer shares (`DEFAULT_PACKAGES`: `@dxos/*`, Effect, React, ...) external, as bare
  imports, and Composer's import map fills them in. All plugins therefore share one copy of each
  package, at the version Composer was built with.
- **EDGE.** A Dynamic Worker has no import map, and Worker Loader resolves a bare specifier against
  the importing file's directory, never against a package registry. So the build also writes a
  `vendor/` directory into the bundle: one build of every package it kept external, and of every
  shared package those import, built for `workerd`, at the version the plugin was built with, and
  `vendor/manifest.json` mapping each specifier to its file. Vendored packages keep importing each
  other bare, so `vendor/manifest.json` is an import map. When compute-service loads the bundle, it
  rewrites each bare import in the bundle, `vendor/` included, to the file the map names. That is a
  textual rewrite of import specifiers, not a second build: no plugin code is recompiled.

The browser ignores `vendor/`; EDGE never loads a module whose `environments` exclude `workerd`, so
browser-only modules and their stylesheets are in the bundle but are never read.

## Bundle format

A plugin bundle is a directory of files that a host loads as is. `composerPlugin` writes it,
`dx registry publish` uploads it, the browser and EDGE load it, and `check-bundle`
(`@dxos/plugin-host-cloudflare`) checks it. This is what the vite build of `plugin-markdown`
emits: 130 files at the root (0.7 MiB of code) and a 37 MiB `vendor/` of 282 modules for 155
specifiers.

```
dist/plugin/
├── manifest.json                      what every host loads the plugin from (see below)
├── index.mjs                          the compiled dx.config.ts; shared code bodies import, never loaded as the entry
├── operation-handler-varxbE3e.mjs     module body, environments [browser, node, tauri, workerd]
├── schema-D3Oofm5R.mjs                module body, adapter @dxos/app-toolkit/SchemaAdapter
├── react-surface-L_9dYgXp.mjs         module body, environments [browser, tauri]
├── chunk-MarkdownOperation-Dw8KGlKE.mjs  code shared between bodies
├── *.mjs.map                          source maps; never loaded, never in the manifest
└── vendor/                            EDGE only
    ├── manifest.json                  the import map: specifier → file, and the build conditions
    ├── effect.mjs, effect/Schema.mjs, …
    ├── @dxos/echo.mjs, @dxos/echo/Obj.mjs, …
    ├── @dxos/plugin-host-cloudflare.mjs   PluginHost, when the build is given its entry
    ├── chunk-22FD6CXA.mjs, …          code shared between vendored packages
    └── asset-automerge_wasm_bg-X4OLCNJ7.wasm
```

### Files

1. **Flat root.** The plugin's own files sit at the bundle root as `<name>-<hash>.mjs`, ESM, and
   import each other relatively (`./chunk-…mjs`). Nothing of the plugin lives in a subdirectory.
2. **No entry module.** A host builds the plugin from `manifest.json` alone (`PluginManifest.toDefinition`,
   `Plugin.fromManifest`) and imports each module body by its `url` when the module activates. `index.mjs`
   remains as the compiled `dx.config.ts`, because bodies import `meta` from it, but no host imports it to
   get the plugin. Only a plugin built from `src/plugin.tsx`, whose manifest lists no modules, still loads
   through `index.mjs`.
3. **Module bodies.** Each `Plugin.config` module is its own chunk, named after its source file.
   Code two bodies share goes to a `chunk-*` file. A body's `environments` say which hosts run it;
   all bodies are in the one bundle.
4. **CommonJS shim.** Every chunk starts with a banner that imports `react`, `react-dom` and
   `react/jsx-runtime` and defines a module-local `require` for bundled CommonJS code.
5. **Stylesheets and assets** sit beside the chunks that import them; only browser-only bodies may
   reach them.

### `manifest.json`

The plugin's `Config2.Plugin` metadata flat at the top level (`key`, `name`, `description`,
`author`, `source`, `icon`, `tags`, `screenshots`, `dependsOn`), plus:

| Field          | Content                                                                                 |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `version`      | The package version.                                                                    |
| `dependencies` | Resolved versions of the packages the plugin depends on.                                |
| `assets`       | Every file of the bundle except source maps and `vendor/`, for the browser's offline cache; `index.mjs` is required only when `modules` is absent. |
| `modules`      | One `ManifestModule` per module, in `Plugin.config` order.                              |

A `ManifestModule` is `{ id, url, requires, provides, activatesOn?, environments?, adapter? }`:
`url` is the body's file (`./<name>-<hash>.mjs`); `requires` and `provides` are capability tags
`{ identifier, arity }`; `activatesOn` is the activation event as data; an absent `environments`
means every host; `adapter.url` is a bare specifier of a shared package
(`@dxos/app-toolkit/SurfaceAdapter`) that turns the body's export into a module.

### `vendor/manifest.json`

```json
{
  "specifiers": { "effect": "effect.mjs", "@dxos/echo/Obj": "@dxos/echo/Obj.mjs" },
  "conditions": ["workerd", "worker", "browser", "import", "module", "default"]
}
```

`specifiers` maps a bare specifier to its file, relative to `vendor/`. `conditions` are the package
export conditions `vendor/` was built with.

### Rules

1. **Shared packages stay bare.** A plugin file imports every package in Composer's shared set
   (`DEFAULT_PACKAGES`) by its bare specifier and never bundles a copy.
2. **`vendor/` holds what EDGE loads.** It holds each shared package that a body running on
   `workerd` reaches, and every shared package those import; browser- and node-only bodies need not
   resolve on EDGE.
3. **Vendored packages import each other bare,** never a copy, so `vendor/manifest.json` is an
   import map. A `require` keeps its package inside the vendored file.
4. **Self-contained.** Every bare import a `workerd` host can reach is in `vendor/manifest.json`, and
   every relative import and manifest URL names a file of the bundle. `vendor/` imports nothing
   outside itself but `node:*` and `cloudflare:*`.
5. **Environments hold statically.** A module that runs on `workerd` never imports a browser-only
   body or a stylesheet statically.
6. **Built once.** No per-runtime build: no `dx-env` entry and no `workerd`, `node`, `tauri` or
   `browser` condition on a plugin's `dx.config.ts` export or on an import that picks a `workerd`
   variant (pkg-lint `plugin-runtime-condition`). A runtime-specific body is its own module with
   its `environments`.

### Loading

- **Browser.** Composer fetches `manifest.json`, builds the plugin from its modules and imports each
  body, through the plugin asset cache, when its module activates. Composer's import map supplies every
  bare specifier at the version Composer was built with; `vendor/` is never read.
- **CLI.** `dx` reads the installed bundle's `manifest.json` the same way and imports each body from
  the install directory when its module activates.
- **EDGE.** PluginLoader puts the bundle and the bundles of its `dependsOn` closure (under
  `deps/<n>/`) in one Worker Loader module map, joins their `vendor/manifest.json` maps into one
  import map (the primary's entry wins), rewrites every bare import in every file to a relative path
  by it, drops vendored files nothing reaches, and adds a main module that starts `PluginHost` from
  `vendor/`. The main module embeds each bundle's `manifest.json` with one literal `import()` per module
  body and adapter (Worker Loader cannot resolve a computed specifier), and `PluginHost.fromManifest`
  builds each plugin from them. Worker Loader resolves a bare specifier against the importing file's directory, never a
  package registry, which is why the rewrite is needed. The rewrite changes only specifiers; no
  code is recompiled.

## Plugin interface

A built plugin meets EDGE at three boundaries. Plugin code sees only the first.

### Module body and host

The app-framework contract, the same as in the browser. A module body's default export is a
`Capability.makeModule(...)`: an Effect that returns capability contributions. `PluginHost` activates
the bundle's plugins with `Environment.current` set to `workerd`, so only modules whose `environments`
include `workerd` load, on the usual events (Startup, Idle, AssistantStart). EDGE reads four
contributions: operation handlers (served by `invoke`), schemas (catalog types), skill definitions
(catalog skills) and layer specs (services the handlers get).

### Host and EDGE

The bundle's main module exports a `WorkerEntrypoint` that implements `PluginProtocol.Entrypoint` from
`@dxos/protocols`, the one contract both repositories compile against. EDGE calls it over Workers RPC;
there is no HTTP protocol.

```ts
interface Entrypoint {
  describe(): Promise<Catalog>; // { plugin, dependencies, operations, skills, types, failures }
  invoke(request: InvokeRequest, scope?: InvocationScope): Promise<InvokeResult>;
}

type InvokeRequest = { operationKey: string; input?: unknown; spaceId?: string };
type InvocationScope = { accessTokenService?: AccessTokenService };
type InvokeResult = { ok: true; output: unknown } | { ok: false; error: Failure };
type Failure = {
  code: 'operation-not-found' | 'invalid-request' | 'failed';
  message: string;
  name?: string;
  stack?: string;
};
```

- **Failures are data.** Workers RPC drops an error's class and stack, so `invoke` returns them in
  `InvokeResult` rather than throwing. `describe` rejects only when the host itself cannot start;
  modules that fail to activate are listed in `Catalog.failures`.
- **`env`** holds the space-agnostic bindings `DATA_SERVICE`, `QUEUE_SERVICE` and
  `FUNCTIONS_AI_SERVICE` (`PluginProtocol.Env`). Everything that differs per call travels in `scope`
  ([D-6]).
- **Operation keys** may be bare or DXNs; they are matched in bare form ([D-22]).
- **Isolation.** The entrypoint serves only the primary plugin's operations, never a dependency's
  ([D-21]).

### EDGE and Cloudflare

PluginLoader calls `LOADER.get(id, () => WorkerCode)` with the bundle's content hash as `id`. The
`WorkerCode` holds the rewritten module map ([Bundle format](#bundle-format)), the generated main
module, compatibility date `2026-03-17` and flags `nodejs_compat`, `nodejs_als` and
`no_handle_cross_request_promise_resolution`, the `env` above, and `globalOutbound: null`: the isolate
reaches the network only through its bindings.

## Architecture

| Component          | Lives in                             | Role                                                                          |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `PluginBuilder`    | dxos: app-framework (`composerPlugin`) | The plugin's vite build: bundle, manifest and `vendor/`.                    |
| `PluginStore`      | edge: registry-service               | The existing `/upload` store, plus an index of operation keys to versions.    |
| `PluginRouter`     | edge: compute-service                | Resolves an operation key to the plugin version that implements it.          |
| `PluginLoader`     | edge: compute-service                | Rewrites bare imports to `vendor/` and loads the bundle into a Dynamic Worker. |
| `PluginHost`       | dxos: `@dxos/plugin-host-cloudflare` | Shipped in `vendor/`: activates the plugin's workerd modules, serves RPC.     |
| `PluginSupervisor` | edge: compute-service                | A Durable Object that runs a plugin's durable module as a facet (phase 3).    |

An invocation of `org.dxos.operation.markdown.create` in space `S` flows:

1. A caller (MCP `invokeOperation`, a trigger, an agent, the Composer client) asks compute-service to
   invoke `dxn:org.dxos.operation.markdown.create` in `S`.
2. `PluginRouter` looks the key up in the operation index and gets the newest version that
   implements it, `org.dxos.plugin.markdown@0.9.2`, with content hash `h`.
3. `PluginLoader` calls `LOADER.get("plugin:h", load)`. On a cold isolate `load` reads the bundle
   from R2, rewrites its bare imports to `vendor/`, adds a main module that starts `PluginHost`,
   and returns the `WorkerCode`; on a warm one nothing is read.
4. compute-service calls `PluginEntrypoint.invoke(request, scope)` on the stub. `scope` is an RPC
   target that carries `S`, the caller, the trace sink and cancellation for this one call.
5. `PluginHost` activates the plugin's `workerd` modules once per isolate, finds the handler, runs it
   with an ECHO client for `S` that `scope` grants, and returns the result.

## Dependencies

```mdl
uses org.dxos.echo: Plugin types are ECHO schemas, and handlers read and write a space through its Database.
```

## Extensions

| Term       | URI                          |
| ---------- | ---------------------------- |
| `type`     | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`      |
| `service`  | `org.dxos.mdl.service@1.0`   |
| `op`       | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`        |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`      |
| `scenario` | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`  |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0`  |
| `uses`     | `dxos.spec.core/uses`        |

## Types

```mdl
type PluginBundle
  desc: |
    One published version of one plugin: the output of its vite build, uploaded as is through
    `/upload` and stored in R2 under `modules/<slug>/<version>/`. Immutable once uploaded. The same
    bundle is loaded by the browser and by EDGE; see the Bundle format section.
  fields:
    pluginKey: NSID              # Config2.Plugin.key, e.g. org.dxos.plugin.markdown
    version: SemVer
    hash: string                 # sha-256 over the bundle's files; the Worker Loader id
    owner: DID | realm:dxos      # from the ATProto release record, or PrivatePlugin.ownerDid
    manifest: Plugin.Manifest    # manifest.json
    catalog: PluginCatalog       # from describe() during publish; TODO: written at build time
    vendor: VendorMap            # vendor/manifest.json
```

```mdl
type VendorMap
  desc: |
    `vendor/manifest.json`: an import map. Every specifier the plugin's files import bare, and every
    shared package a vendored package imports, mapped to the file under `vendor/` that PluginLoader
    rewrites the import to. A bare import with no entry, in the plugin or in `vendor/`, is a build
    error, not a runtime one.
  fields:
    specifiers: Record<string, string>   # e.g. "@dxos/echo/Obj" → "@dxos/echo/Obj.mjs"
    conditions: string[]                 # the conditions vendor/ was built with, e.g. [workerd, worker, import]
```

```mdl
type PluginCatalog
  desc: |
    What a bundle contributes on EDGE: what `describe()` returns after activating its workerd modules
    (the same activation `activateHeadlessPlugins` runs: Startup, Idle, AssistantStart). Produced
    during publish; TODO: written at build time. Lets the registry, the router and MCP answer queries
    without starting an isolate.
  fields:
    operations: PersistentOperation[]   # key, name, description, inputSchema, outputSchema, services
    skills: SkillRecord[]               # key, name, description, instructions, tools
    types: TypeRecord[]                 # typename, version, JSON Schema of each AppCapabilities.Schema entry
```

```mdl
type OperationRoute
  desc: |
    One row of the operation index: which plugin version implements an operation key. A key belongs
    to exactly one plugin key; each published version adds rows, and the router picks the newest.
  fields:
    operationKey: NSID
    pluginKey: NSID
    version: SemVer
    hash: string
```

```mdl
type InvocationScope
  desc: |
    The per-call capability compute-service passes as an RPC argument to `PluginEntrypoint.invoke`.
    Everything that differs between two calls to the same isolate lives here, never in `env`, so one
    isolate serves every space.
  fields:
    spaceId: SpaceId
    caller: Actor
    traceSink: RpcTarget
    cancellation: AbortSignal
    database(): EchoDatabase    # scoped to spaceId; built on DATA_SERVICE and QUEUE_SERVICE
    accessToken(source): string # through the props-bound AccessTokenService
    invoke(key, input): unknown # nested operation, re-enters compute-service
```

## Services

```mdl
service PluginBuilder
  desc: |
    The plugin's own vite build (`composerPlugin`), with no separate build for EDGE. It emits the
    plugin's files flat at the bundle root, `manifest.json` with every module and its
    `environments`, and `vendor/`: one build, for `workerd`, of every package the plugin build kept
    external, with `vendor/manifest.json`. TODO: write the bundle's catalog at build time; until then
    it is produced by `describe()` during publish.
  ops:
    build(configPath: string, options: { version }) → PluginBundle
  errors:
    UnvendoredImport: a plugin file imports a bare specifier that vendor/ does not provide
```

```mdl
service PluginStore
  desc: |
    registry-service's existing plugin store (`/upload`, `/private/upload`, `GET /modules/...`). On
    upload it loads the bundle once through Worker Loader and calls `describe()`, which is how publish
    produces the bundle's catalog until the build writes it (TODO); it stores the catalog and adds
    the bundle's operation keys to the operation index. A bundle whose `describe()` fails is refused.
  ops:
    upload(bundle: PluginBundle) → PluginBundle
    getManifest(pluginKey: NSID, version?: SemVer) → Plugin.Manifest
    getCatalog(pluginKey: NSID, version?: SemVer) → PluginCatalog
    query(filter: { text?, pluginKey?, operationKey?, typename?, skillKey? }) → PluginBundle[]
    resolveOperation(operationKey: NSID) → OperationRoute
    revoke(pluginKey: NSID, version: SemVer) → void
  errors:
    VersionExists: the version is uploaded with a different hash
    KeyConflict: an operation key in the catalog belongs to another plugin key
    NotOwner: the uploader is not the plugin key's owner
```

```mdl
service PluginLoader
  desc: |
    compute-service's Worker Loader client for plugin bundles. Uses `LOADER.get` with the bundle hash
    as the id, so one warm isolate serves every space and every caller of that version, and the
    callback reads R2 only on a cold start. The callback adds the bundles of the plugin's `dependsOn`
    closure beside it, joins their VendorMaps into one import map (the primary's entry wins, so the
    isolate holds one copy of each package), rewrites every bare import by it, drops vendored files
    nothing reaches, and adds a main module that starts `PluginHost` from `vendor/`. `env` holds only space-agnostic
    bindings: DATA_SERVICE, QUEUE_SERVICE, an AI service and loopback entrypoints from `ctx.exports`
    whose props name the plugin, never a space. `tails` routes logs to FunctionsLoggerEntrypoint.
  loading: lazy
  ops:
    entrypoint(route: OperationRoute) → PluginEntrypoint
  errors:
    PluginLoadError: the bundle failed to load or to activate; carries the plugin version and the cause
```

```mdl
service PluginEntrypoint
  desc: |
    The RPC surface `PluginHost` exports from inside the Dynamic Worker, as a WorkerEntrypoint
    implementing `PluginProtocol.Entrypoint` (see the Plugin interface section). It serves only the
    primary plugin: its `dependsOn` closure is activated for the capabilities the primary uses, and
    those plugins' own operations, skills and types belong to their own bundles. Failures are returned
    as data in `InvokeResult`, since Workers RPC drops an error's class and stack.
  ops:
    describe() → PluginCatalog
    invoke(request: { operationKey, input, spaceId? }, scope: InvocationScope) → InvokeResult
  errors:
    OperationNotFound: the primary plugin has no handler for the key (code `operation-not-found`)
```

```mdl
service PluginRouter
  desc: |
    compute-service's resolution of a `dxn:<operation key>[@<version>]` target to the newest version
    that implements it, from PluginStore's operation index, falling back to operation-service while
    operation-service still runs ([F-6.6]). TODO: a per-space setting, with an override per DXN,
    picks another version.
  ops:
    route(target: string) → OperationRoute
  errors:
    OperationNotFound: no published version implements the key
```

```mdl
service PluginSupervisor
  desc: |
    A SQLite Durable Object, one per (space, plugin key), that runs a plugin's durable module as a
    Durable Object facet: `ctx.facets.get(<module id>, () => ({ class:
    worker.getDurableObjectClass(<export>) }))`. The facet owns its own SQLite database; the
    supervisor keeps the bundle hash, access control and billing counters in its own, which the
    facet cannot read. An upgrade aborts the facet and restarts it with the new version's class;
    its storage survives.
  ops:
    start(spaceId: SpaceId, pluginKey: NSID) → FacetStub
    upgrade(hash: string) → void
    delete() → void
```

## Operations

```mdl
op publishPlugin
  desc: |
    CLI `dx registry publish [--private]`, the command that already publishes a plugin's bundle: it
    runs the plugin's vite build with `DX_PLUGIN_HOST_ENTRY` naming the CLI's `PluginHost`, so the
    build vendors the host whatever the plugin's own vite config says, loads the bundle in a local
    workerd and calls `describe()`, and
    uploads the one bundle, `vendor/` included, through `/upload` with a verifiable presentation, API
    token or admin key; PluginStore calls `describe()` again on upload. Publishing fails when either
    call fails. Nothing is built or uploaded separately for EDGE.
  status: unimplemented
  input:
    configPath: string
  output: PluginBundle
  errors:
    VersionExists: the version is already uploaded with another hash
    KeyConflict: the catalog claims an operation key owned by another plugin
    PluginLoadError: the bundle failed to load or activate when the registry described it
```

```mdl
op queryPlugins
  desc: |
    Registry query over published plugins, by text, plugin key, operation key, typename or skill key.
    Exposed as `GET /registry/plugins` and as an operation so agents can call it.
  status: unimplemented
  input:
    text?: string
    operationKey?: NSID
    typename?: string
    skillKey?: NSID
  output: PluginBundle[]
```

```mdl
op invokeOperation
  desc: |
    The existing `ComputeServiceEntrypoint.invokeOperation`, with `dxn:` targets routed by
    PluginRouter to a PluginEntrypoint instead of to operation-service.
  input:
    key: NSID
    input: unknown
    spaceId: SpaceId
    version?: SemVer
  output: unknown
  errors:
    OperationNotFound: no published version implements the key
    PluginLoadError: the bundle failed to load
  requires: [PluginRouter, PluginLoader]
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Publish a plugin from the CLI
  `dx registry publish` publishes a plugin's one bundle, which the browser and EDGE both load.

  req F-1.1:
    when: a developer runs `dx registry publish` in a plugin package
    then: the plugin's vite build emits one bundle with one manifest, and the bundle is uploaded to
          PluginStore as one R2 prefix

  req F-1.2: A plugin package builds no separate bundle per runtime: no `dx-env` build of
    `dx.config.ts`, and no `workerd`, `node`, `tauri` or `browser` condition on its `dx.config.ts`
    export. A module's `environments` decide at load whether it runs.

  req F-1.3:
    when: a bundle is uploaded
    then: registry-service loads it in a fresh isolate and stores what `describe()` returns as its
          catalog; a bundle that fails to load or activate is refused

  req F-1.4: The bundle carries `vendor/`, so a Dynamic Worker loads it from its `modules` map alone:
    after PluginLoader's rewrite, its files import nothing but each other, `vendor/` and
    `cloudflare:*`.

  req F-1.5:
    when: a plugin file imports a bare specifier that `vendor/` does not provide
    then: the build fails with UnvendoredImport and nothing is uploaded

  req F-1.6: Upload is authorized by the plugin key's owner (DID or realm:dxos); the first upload of
    a key sets its owner.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Versioning
  Bundles are immutable and addressed by plugin key, semver and content hash.

  req F-2.1: A (plugin key, version) pair holds one bundle; uploading it again with another hash
    fails with VersionExists, with the same hash it is a no-op.

  req F-2.2: An invocation runs the newest published version that implements its operation key, the
    rule the browser registry uses for the version it offers.

  req F-2.3: An invocation may name a version (`dxn:<key>@<version>`); it then runs that version.

  req F-2.4: Revoking a version removes its operation index rows and stops new invocations; the next
    newest version takes its place.

  req F-2.5: The isolate id is the bundle hash, so a changed bundle never reuses an isolate of the
    old one and an unchanged bundle always can.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Types are exposed
  The ECHO types a plugin contributes are visible on EDGE and usable by its operations.

  req F-3.1: A bundle's catalog lists the JSON Schema of every `AppCapabilities.Schema` entry of its
    workerd modules.

  req F-3.2: PluginHost registers those types in the space registry of every invocation, so a
    handler needs no per-operation `types`.

  req F-3.3: PluginStore.query finds the bundle that contributes a typename.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Skills are exposed
  The skills a plugin contributes are listed on EDGE and loadable over MCP.

  req F-4.1: A bundle's catalog lists every `AppCapabilities.SkillDefinition`, with resolved
    instructions and its tool keys.

  req F-4.2: The MCP server's `loadSkill` and the skill list of compute-service's agents read skills
    from the union of the newest bundles' catalogs.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Operations are exposed and callable
  Every operation a published plugin handles can be found and invoked on EDGE.

  req F-5.1: The MCP server's `queryOperations` lists the operations of the newest version of every
    published plugin.

  req F-5.2:
    when: compute-service receives `invokeOperation` for a key a published plugin implements
    then: the operation runs in that plugin's Dynamic Worker, in the scope of the given space, and
          its result or typed error is returned to the caller

  req F-5.3: A handler's nested `Operation.invoke` re-enters compute-service through a service
    binding, so a call chain across plugins never holds more than one Dynamic Worker per I/O context.

  req F-5.4: An operation key belongs to exactly one plugin key; an upload whose catalog claims a key
    of another plugin fails with KeyConflict.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: compute-service routes invocations to published plugins
  compute-service is the single entry for running plugin code on EDGE.

  req F-6.1: PluginRouter resolves a `dxn:` target from the operation index without starting an
    isolate.

  req F-6.2: PluginLoader loads a bundle with `LOADER.get(hash, ...)`; `env` holds no space id,
    caller or token, and the per-call InvocationScope arrives as an RPC argument.

  req F-6.3: A bundle that fails to load or activate returns PluginLoadError naming the plugin
    version; it does not affect other plugins.

  req F-6.4: Triggers, processes, agents and code-mode scripts invoke plugin operations through the
    same route as `invokeOperation`.

  req F-6.5: Logs and traces of a Dynamic Worker reach the same sinks as other compute-service
    functions, tagged with the plugin key and version.

  req F-6.6: While both run, PluginRouter prefers a published plugin and falls back to
    operation-service for keys no published plugin implements.

  req F-6.7: operation-service is removed once every plugin in its static list is published, and no
    caller binds `OPERATION_SERVICE`.

  req F-6.8: The KV operation catalog is rebuilt from bundle catalogs on every upload or revoke, not
    by a cron that activates plugins.
```

```mdl
feat F-8: Durable plugin state through facets
  A plugin module that needs its own storage runs as a Durable Object facet.

  req F-8.1: A module that exports a Durable Object class runs as a facet of a PluginSupervisor, one
    per (space, plugin key), with a SQLite database the supervisor cannot read and the facet cannot
    leave.

  req F-8.2: Upgrading the plugin aborts the facet and restarts it with the new class; its storage
    is kept.

  req F-8.3: Uninstalling the plugin from a space deletes its facets and their storage.
```

```mdl
feat F-9: Continuous delivery from DXOS
  First-party plugins reach EDGE from the dxos repository without a catalog bump.

  req F-9.1: A dxos release publishes every first-party plugin whose bundle changed, at the released
    version, owned by realm:dxos.

  req F-9.2: The workflow runs in dxos CI with an admin credential; edge needs no redeploy.
```

```mdl
feat F-10: Readable manifests and registry query
  Anyone can read what a published plugin contains before running it.

  req F-10.1: `GET /registry/modules/:slug/:version/manifest.json` returns the bundle's manifest, and
    `GET /registry/plugins/:key/:version/catalog` the catalog the registry derived at upload.

  req F-10.2: queryPlugins finds published plugins by text, plugin key, operation key, typename or
    skill key.

  req F-10.3: Private plugins are returned only to their owner and to members of spaces they are
    shared with.
```

```mdl
feat F-12: Tested end to end
  Every publishable plugin, the host and the EDGE path are tested in the runtime they run in.

  req F-12.1: CI loads the bundle of each plugin tagged `edge-release` through Worker Loader in
    workerd, as PluginLoader does, and fails when an import is not vendored, a workerd module fails
    to activate, or the plugin contributes no operations, skills or types.

  req F-12.2: The host is tested inside workerd: describe, invoke, dependency attribution and errors.

  req F-12.3: EDGE end-to-end tests publish a bundle, route and invoke its operations in a real space,
    and cover scenarios T-1 to T-4, upgrade and revocation.
```

## Scenarios

```mdl
scenario T-1: Publish and call a plugin
  given: plugin-markdown at version 0.9.2, never published
  when: a developer runs `dx registry publish`, then MCP invokeOperation calls
        org.dxos.operation.markdown.create in space S
  then:
    - registry-service holds one bundle for org.dxos.plugin.markdown@0.9.2, which the browser loads too
    - queryOperations lists org.dxos.operation.markdown.create
    - the document is created in S by the Dynamic Worker, not by operation-service
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: One isolate serves two spaces
  given: a published bundle with hash h
  when: the same operation is invoked in space S1, then in space S2
  then:
    - both calls use the Worker Loader id `plugin:h`
    - each handler sees only its own space's database
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: Upgrade a plugin
  given: version 0.9.2 is the newest published version
  when: 0.9.3 is published
  then:
    - new invocations run 0.9.3 in a new isolate
    - an invocation naming @0.9.2 still runs 0.9.2
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: Operation key conflict
  given: org.dxos.plugin.markdown owns org.dxos.operation.markdown.create
  when: another plugin key uploads a catalog that contains the same operation key
  then:
    - the upload fails with KeyConflict
    - the operation index is unchanged
```

## Versions

Which version runs when the caller does not name one: the newest published version that
implements the operation key, by semver, the same rule the browser registry uses for the version
it offers. An explicit version (`dxn:<key>@<version>`) wins. There are no channels. TODO: a
per-space setting, with an override per DXN, picks a version for a space.

## Rollout

1. **One bundle.** Plugin builds emit one bundle, with `vendor/`; the per-runtime
   `dx-env` builds are removed ([F-1]).
2. **Store and index.** registry-service indexes the operation keys of uploaded bundles and serves
   their catalogs ([F-2], [F-10]).
3. **Load and route.** compute-service routes `dxn:` targets to published plugins, with
   operation-service as the fallback; dxos releases publish first-party plugins ([F-5], [F-6], [F-9]).
4. **Retire operation-service** once every plugin in its list is published ([F-6.7]).
5. **Durable state** ([F-8]).

## Open questions

- **Isolate reuse.** compute-service gives every function invocation a random loader id today, to
  work around WASM memory growth. [F-2.5] needs the reuse that workaround disables; the cause has to
  be fixed or bounded per bundle.
- **Bundle size.** Each bundle carries its own `vendor/` copy of `@dxos/echo`, Effect and the rest.
  Its size and cold-start cost, and any Worker Loader size limit, need measuring against
  operation-service's 3.9 MiB gzip; identical vendor files could be stored once by content hash.
- **Concurrency.** A Durable Object may have 10 Dynamic Workers in flight, a Worker request 4. An
  agent process that calls operations of many plugins must spread them across I/O contexts.
- **Sandboxing options.** Whether a plugin's egress, CPU and subrequest limits become configurable.
  Any such option applies to every plugin alike, whoever publishes it.
- **Facet scope.** Whether one supervisor per (space, plugin) is the right grain, or per plugin
  instance object.
- **Data-plane compatibility.** `vendor/` carries the ECHO client the plugin was built with, so a
  plugin opens a space only when that client speaks the protocol of the db-service it runs
  against. A bundle built from dxos `main` one day ahead of the dxos EDGE pins already timed out
  opening a space root. Whether a manifest records the SDK version it was built from, and
  compute-service refuses a bundle the data plane cannot serve, or the data service protocol is
  versioned instead.
- **Dependencies across bundles.** PluginLoader resolves a package both bundles vendor to the
  primary's copy, as Composer's import map serves one version to every plugin; that is safe only
  while the two versions are compatible. Whether a bundle records the version range it accepts.

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: The same plugins that run in Composer run on EDGE. Plugins are deployed from the CLI and stored on EDGE; they are versioned; their types and skills are exposed; their operations are exposed and callable; compute-service executes and routes invocations to deployed plugins; operation-service is deprecated and removed; first-party plugins are delivered continuously from DXOS; and plugin manifests and the registry can be read and queried.
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1, F-2, F-3, F-4, F-5, F-6, F-7, F-9, F-10]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: EDGE loads plugin code with the Worker Loader API, and potentially with Durable Object facets. The design builds on the `Plugin.config` definition and manifest of dxos/dxos#13893.
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [PluginLoader, PluginSupervisor, EdgeManifest]
  source: https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/13893
```

```mdl
decision D-3: An EDGE release is the `dx-env=workerd` build of a plugin's `dx.config.ts`, bundled with its `dependsOn` plugins and a generated PluginHost entry into one self-contained module graph. Fills in [D-1] and [D-2].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [EdgeRelease, EdgeManifest, PluginBuilder, F-1.4]
  why: A Dynamic Worker resolves imports only from its own `modules` map, so a release cannot share `@dxos/*` modules with the host or with other releases; bundling the dependsOn closure lets the plugin activate with the capabilities it requires.
  rejected:
    - One isolate for all plugins, rebuilt on each deploy — it keeps operation-service's coupling, where one plugin's change reloads and can break every other.
    - Ship `@dxos/*` as separate shared modules in each WorkerCode — the bytes are loaded per isolate either way, and version skew between releases becomes possible.
```

```mdl
decision D-4: Each release carries a catalog of its operations, skills and types, written at build time by activating the release headless; registry, router and MCP read the catalog and never start an isolate to answer a query. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [PluginCatalog, F-3, F-4, F-10, F-1.3]
  why: Readable manifests and registry query need the contributions as data; checking `describe()` against the catalog at upload keeps the two from drifting.
  rejected:
    - Activate releases on a cron to rebuild the catalog, as operation-service does — it costs an isolate per release per minute and fails silently.
```

```mdl
decision D-5: Releases are stored by registry-service beside the browser bundle of the same PluginRelease (R2 prefix `workerd/`, one D1 row), are immutable per (key, version), and sit on a channel (stable, next, dev); the uploader of a key's first release owns the key. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [EdgeRelease, PluginStore, F-2]
  why: registry-service already owns plugin bundles, versions and private releases; a second store would split one plugin's identity in two.
  rejected:
    - Store releases in compute-service's functions registry — it models single functions with no manifest, types or skills.
```

```mdl
decision D-6: The Worker Loader id of a release is its content hash, shared by every space and caller; per-call state (space, caller, trace sink, cancellation) reaches the isolate as an InvocationScope RPC argument, never in `env`. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [PluginLoader, InvocationScope, F-2.5, F-6.2]
  why: Worker Loader caches and bills by (id, code); `env` is part of the code, so per-space env would start and bill one isolate per space.
  rejected:
    - A random id per invocation, as functions use today — no warm isolates, and a new billed Dynamic Worker on every call.
    - One id per (release, space) — a cold start and a billed worker per space with no isolation gain, since InvocationScope already scopes data access.
```

```mdl
decision D-7: compute-service resolves `dxn:` operation targets through an operation index that maps each key to exactly one plugin key, falling back to operation-service until it is removed; nested invocations re-enter compute-service through a service binding. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [PluginRouter, OperationRoute, F-5.3, F-5.4, F-6, F-7.1]
  why: The index lets routing skip isolate start-up; exclusive key ownership keeps a third-party plugin from shadowing a first-party operation; re-entry keeps each call within the per-I/O-context Dynamic Worker limit.
```

```mdl
decision D-8: Durable Object facets host only modules that need their own storage, one PluginSupervisor per (space, plugin key); stateless operations run in a plain WorkerEntrypoint. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [PluginSupervisor, F-8]
  why: Facets add a Durable Object hop and are in beta; operations hold their state in ECHO and need neither.
```

```mdl
decision D-9: First-party plugins are delivered by a dxos CI workflow that deploys changed releases to `dev` on merge to main and to `stable` on a dxos release, as realm:dxos. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9]
  why: The release hash tells CI which plugins changed, so edge needs no catalog bump or redeploy.
```

```mdl
decision D-10: Plugins not owned by realm:dxos load with a filtered `globalOutbound` that allows only the hostnames their manifest declares, with custom CPU and subrequest limits. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [EdgeManifest, F-11]
  why: Deploying from the CLI opens EDGE to code DXOS did not write; egress and limits are the controls Worker Loader offers.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: DXOS plugins get no permissions that other plugins lack; drop the sandboxing that applied only to plugins not owned by realm:dxos ([F-11]).
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  supersedes: D-10
  affects: [EdgeManifest]
```

```mdl
decision D-12: Putting a plugin on EDGE is publishing, not deploying: it pushes static assets up to a service.
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [publishPlugin, F-1]
```

```mdl
decision D-13: `dx registry publish`, which already publishes the browser bundle, publishes the `workerd` release of the same version with it; there is no separate deploy command. Fills in [D-12].
  date: 2026-10-29
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
